DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     This program manages “Unified Extensible Firmware Interface” (UEFI) envi‐
     ronment variables.  UEFI variables have three parts: A namespace, a name
     and a value.  The namespace is a GUID that is self assigned by the group
     defining the variables.  The name is a Unicode name for the variable.
     The value is binary data.  All Unicode data is presented to the user as
     UTF-8.

     The following options are available:

     --nn _n_a_m_e ----nnaammee _n_a_m_e   Specify the name of the variable to operate on.
                           The _n_a_m_e argument is the GUID of the variable, fol‐
                           lowed by a dash, followed by the UEFI variable
                           name.  The GUID may be in numeric format, or may be
                           one of the well known symbolic names (see
                           ----lliisstt--gguuiiddss for a complete list).

     --ff _f_i_l_e ----ffrroommffiillee _f_i_l_e
                           When writing or appending to a variable, take the
                           data for the variable's value from _f_i_l_e instead of
                           from the command line.  This flag implies ----wwrriittee
                           unless the ----aappppeenndd or ----pprriinntt flags are given.
                           This behavior is not well understood and is cur‐
                           rently unimplemented for writes.  When ----pprriinntt is
                           specified, the contents of the file are used as the
                           value to print using any other specified flags.
                           This is used primarily for testing purposes for
                           more complicated variable decoding.

     --aa ----aappppeenndd           Append the specified value to the UEFI variable
                           rather than replacing it.

     --tt _a_t_t_r ----aattttrriibbuutteess _a_t_t_r
                           Specify, in hexadecimal, the attributes for this
                           variable.  See section 7.2 (GetVariable subsection,
                           Related Definitions) of the UEFI Specification for
                           hex values to use.

     --AA ----aasscciiii            Display the variable data as modified ASCII: All
                           printable characters are printed, while unprintable
                           characters are rendered as a two-digit hexadecimal
                           number preceded by a % character.

     --bb ----bbiinnaarryy           Display the variable data as binary data.  Usually
                           will be used with the --NN or ----nnoo--nnaammee flag.  Useful
                           in scripts.

     --DD ----ddeelleettee           Delete the specified variable.  May not be used
                           with either the ----wwrriittee or the ----aappppeenndd flags.  No
                           _v_a_l_u_e may be specified.

     --dd ----ddeevviiccee ----ddeevviiccee--ppaatthh
                           Interpret the variables printed as UEFI device
                           paths and print the UEFI standard string represen‐
                           tation.

     --gg ----gguuiidd             Convert GUIDs to names if they are known (and show
                           them in ----lliisstt--gguuiiddss).

     --HH ----hheexx              List variable data as a hex dump.

     --LL ----lliisstt--gguuiiddss       Lists the well known GUIDs.  The names listed here
                           may be used in place of the numeric GUID values.
                           These names will replace the numeric GUID values
                           unless the ----rraaww--gguuiidd flag is specified.

     --ll ----lliisstt             List all the variables.  If the ----pprriinntt flag is
                           also listed, their values will be displayed.

     ----llooaadd--ooppttiioonn         Decode the variable as if it were a UEFI Boot Op‐
                           tion, including information about what device
                           and/or paths the UEFI DevicePaths decode to.

     --NN ----nnoo--nnaammee          Do not display the variable name.

     --pp ----pprriinntt            Print the value of the variable.

     --qq ----qquuiieett            When an error occurs, exit with a non-zero value
                           without outputting any error messages.  Otherwise,
                           produce the normal output and exit with a zero sta‐
                           tus.

     --RR ----rraaww--gguuiidd         Do not substitute well known names for GUID numeric
                           values in output.

     --uu ----uuttff88             Treat the value of the variable as UCS2 and convert
                           it to UTF8 and print the result.

     --ww ----wwrriittee            Write (replace) the variable specified with the
                           value specified from standard input.  No command
                           line option to do this is available since UEFI
                           variables are binary structures rather than
                           strings.  echo(1) --nn can be used to specify simple
                           strings.

     _n_a_m_e                  Display the _n_a_m_e environment variable.

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     The eeffiivvaarr program is intended to be compatible (strict superset) with a
     program of the same name included in the Red Hat libefivar package, but
     the --dd and ----pprriinntt--ddeecciimmaall flags are not implemented and never will be.

     The --dd flag is short for ----ddeevviiccee--ppaatthh.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     Appendix A of the UEFI specification has the format for GUIDs.  All GUIDs
     “Globally Unique Identifiers” have the format described in RFC 4122.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The eeffiivvaarr utility first appeared in FreeBSD 11.1.

